# Overview
#
# Each phrase vouches for a fact that the sentence already stated (`It
# does exactly that`, `returns the number, and nothing else`). The
# phrase names nothing that the reader can observe. The fix deletes it.
# The rule sits at Recommended, because docs reuse the same words with a
# plain meaning.
#
# Expression
#
# The pattern pins each phrase to its reassuring sense. `no magic` skips
# a following noun in `magic` (`no magic number`, `no magic bytes`). `no
# surprises` counts only at the end of a clause. A clause is a subject
# and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. `the whole point` and `the
# entire point` need `of`, `is`, `was`, or `that` after them, or `is` or
# `was` before them. `no more and no less` skips a following `than`, and
# so does its comma form (`no more, no less`).
#
# `nothing more, nothing less` never takes `than`, so it counts as
# written. By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope
# "Sentence"` lets the comma forms cross one.
#
# A negated word after a space needs a word to consume (`less !than`),
# so each branch with `!than` or `!@magic` has a twin for the end of a
# clause. `no surprises` and each of those twins carry a list of clause
# punctuation. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so each list
# sits glued to its word (`surprises[., ;, :, (,)]`) and reads the token
# right after it. Each branch spells the list out, because
# `surprises@end` tokenizes as one word. A comma separates list items,
# so a bare comma cannot sit in a list. The pattern wraps it as a
# one-token sequence.
#
# `and nothing else` counts only at the end of a clause. Its guard
# `else!( )` takes any token but whitespace right after the phrase, so
# it also counts at the end of a list item with no period. A line break
# inside a paragraph reads as whitespace, so the guard fails there.
#
# Weir has no escape for a literal paren, so a twin branch names the
# open paren of a citation another way (`and nothing else (see the
# keyword table)`). The twin takes a space and then a token outside
# every part-of-speech tag with no space after it. `tagged` lists the
# tags that Harper's part-of-speech tagger gives a word. The glued token
# keeps out a verb that the tagger left untagged (`and nothing else
# deletes it`), because a space follows a word.
#
# False Positives
#
# Docs reuse the same words with a plain meaning, and the guards keep
# those senses out. `reported no surprises after the migration` is a
# report. `rounds to the whole point` and `the whole point cloud` are
# measurements. `than` turns `no more and no less` into a bound. Before
# a verb, `nothing else` is the subject of a new clause (`touches the
# file and nothing else references it`).
#
# The rule leaves out `without changing it`, because the phrase also
# states a fact that its sentence needs. In `A validating policy accepts
# or rejects an object without changing it`, the phrase separates a
# validating policy from a mutating one, and the fix deletes that fact.
# On a getter, the phrase vouches (`Value returns the current count
# without changing it`), and the rule passes that sentence too. The
# phrase did not hit any line in the measure.py corpus, so no sample
# measures a guard that separates the two senses.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseDeliberate holds the phrases that vouch for a choice instead.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 5 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 5 of 5 sampled
# hits the sentence stated the same fact without the phrase. Two were
# `as the name implies`, two were `the whole point of`, and one was `and
# nothing else`.
expr tagged [ADJ, ADP, ADV, AUX, CCONJ, DET, INTJ, NOUN, NUM, PART, PRON, PROPN, SCONJ, SYM, VERB, X]
expr magic [number, numbers, byte, bytes, prefix, string, strings, value, values, constant, constants]
expr main [(no more and no less !than), (no more and no less[., ;, :, (,)]), (no more, no less !than), (no more, no less[., ;, :, (,)]), (nothing more, nothing less), (nothing more and nothing less), (and nothing else!( )), (and nothing else !@tagged!( )), (exactly what you would expect), (exactly what you'd expect), (no magic !@magic), (no magic[., ;, :, (,)]), (no surprises[., ;, :, (,)]), (as the name [suggests, implies]), (the [whole, entire] point [of, is, was, that]), ([is, was] the [whole, entire] point), (it does exactly that)]
let message "Reassurance. A stated fact does not need vouching, so delete the phrase."
let description "Flags phrases that insist on a fact the sentence already stated."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Close releases the file handle."
allows "The file has no magic bytes, so the loader inspects the extension."
allows "There is no magic-number check."
allows "Users reported no surprises after the migration."
allows "The no surprises policy applies to all releases."
allows "The interest rate rounds to the whole point."
allows "The entire point cloud fits in memory."
allows "The reader reads no more and no less than n bytes, or returns an error."
allows "The reader reads no more, no less than n bytes."
allows "Probe touches the file and nothing else references it."
allows "The prune does not touch it, and nothing else deletes it."
allows "A validating policy accepts or rejects an object without changing it."
